WEBVTT

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.238
A language model has been asked to plan a
route for a robot.

00:00:08.298 --> 00:00:11.632
It answers in seconds, and it sounds
certain.

00:00:11.692 --> 00:00:14.277
And the route goes straight through a
wall.

00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:18.870
Models do this. A goal inside a wall.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:23.423
A jump across a room. A waypoint off the
edge of the map.

00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:27.677
So this project puts a gate between the
model and the motors.

00:00:32.427 --> 00:00:37.301
Every proposed route is checked against
the map, before a wheel turns.

00:00:37.362 --> 00:00:41.049
The checks are plain geometry. No second
model.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:44.549
No judgement. The same answer, every time.

00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:48.928
First. Is every waypoint on the map?

00:00:53.078 --> 00:00:57.370
Second. Is every step short enough to be
one continuous move?

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.916
Third. Does the robot's whole body,

00:01:03.866 --> 00:01:07.652
swept along the route, stay clear of every
obstacle?

00:01:12.002 --> 00:01:15.258
And clear of space that nobody has mapped?

00:01:19.208 --> 00:01:22.949
A route that clears every check goes
through untouched.

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:27.624
Anything else is refused, and the motors
never hear of it.

00:01:27.684 --> 00:01:31.084
A verdict takes about ten millionths of a
second.

00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:38.913
A gate can fail in two directions.

00:01:38.972 --> 00:01:44.014
It can let danger through. Or it can block
a route that was perfectly fine.

00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:46.522
So it is tested in both.

00:01:48.072 --> 00:01:52.723
Fifty generated buildings. Fourteen
hundred and seventy two routes,

00:01:52.673 --> 00:01:56.132
each built to break a rule in one of six
ways.

00:01:56.192 --> 00:01:58.099
Every one was caught.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:03.970
And five hundred and ninety nine routes
known to be safe. Not one was refused.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.654
Then, two real language models.

00:02:11.714 --> 00:02:16.667
Forty buildings each, drawn as text. One
attempt per building.

00:02:16.727 --> 00:02:22.476
The smaller model proposed an unsafe route
thirty five times out of forty.

00:02:22.426 --> 00:02:24.806
The larger one, thirty two.

00:02:24.866 --> 00:02:30.740
The gate caught all sixty seven. It passed
the thirteen safe ones. It missed none.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.056
The larger model is the instructive one.

00:02:36.116 --> 00:02:40.276
Every route it gave started and ended
where it was asked.

00:02:40.337 --> 00:02:44.400
Seventeen of them went through an obstacle
on the way.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:49.468
Unsafe, but on target. That is the failure
a gate exists for.

00:02:54.618 --> 00:02:56.470
Now, the fine print.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.180
This is two models, on forty generated
buildings,

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:04.471
offline. It is not every model, and it is
not hardware.

00:03:04.531 --> 00:03:07.506
An earlier hardware result was withdrawn,

00:03:07.456 --> 00:03:12.535
when the logs behind it were lost. It
returns only with recordings.

00:03:12.595 --> 00:03:15.853
And the gate checks each route once. Maps
move,

00:03:15.803 --> 00:03:18.901
and a route that passed can stop passing.

00:03:20.651 --> 00:03:24.045
ROS2, LLM Safety Verifier.

00:03:24.106 --> 00:03:28.296
A deterministic gate, between the model
and the motors.
